+// ── T9-W7 §10 (M03a) · THE WORDMARK PUBLISHES ITS OWN FOOT ─────────────────────────────────
+// The raised sheet's cap (`scene.css`'s `--sheet-chrome`) is "everything above the sheet that
+// the sheet must not reach", and the wordmark is the bottom of that. It was a constant — 12rem
+// — and the mark it is measured against has been re-scaled twice since; at 390 the case's own
+// 3px stroke had walked 2.73px INSIDE the wordmark's box. So the sheet reads the seam instead
+// of a number: this is the publisher, the derivation is `scene.css`'s, and it is one-sided
+// there so no width loses air it already had.
+// MEASURED, NOT SPELLED, for the reason every measured token in this estate is: the wordmark's
+// box is a scale on a class (`--logo-scale`) times a viewport-dependent layout, and a literal
+// written here would go stale in the one direction that reopens the row. The observer is the
+// wordmark's OWN box (`logoMenu.$el`, the stable element the fold already anchors on), never
+// the `<h1>`'s — the heading is a stretched flex item whose width and height are the layout's,
+// not the ink's (the fold's own finding, above).
+// ── PASS 2 · TWO CORRECTIONS, AND THE SECOND IS THE 7.00px ────────────────────────────────
+// FIRST: the observer watches what it publishes. It read the wordmark's box and watched the
+// MASTHEAD's — two boxes on two clocks, and a subject that is not the thing observed goes stale
+// the moment the two move apart.
+// SECOND, and it is the one the number was hiding: `logoMenu.$el` is the menu's box, and the
+// INK's box is 7.00px shorter inside it (measured at 390×844, sheet up: trigger 71.22 tall, its
+// host 78.22, tops equal). The pass-1 spec booked that 7.00 as staleness; it is not a clock, it
+// is a BOX, and a seam derived from the menu's foot holds its 8px of air against a line the
+// reader cannot see. So the publisher reads the drawn `<svg>` itself and falls back outward —
+// ink, then host, then masthead — so a boot before the logo mounts still publishes.
+function wordmarkInk(): Element | null {
+  const host = wordmarkEl();
+  return host?.querySelector("svg.handwritten-logo") ?? host;
+}
+
+function publishMastheadFoot() {
+  const ink = wordmarkInk();
+  if (!ink) return;
+  document.documentElement.style.setProperty(
+    "--masthead-foot",
+    `${ink.getBoundingClientRect().bottom.toFixed(2)}px`,
+  );
+}
+
+// BOTH boxes are observed and ONE is published, and the pair is the whole subtlety: a
+// ResizeObserver fires on SIZE, and what this token publishes is a POSITION. The ink can keep
+// its size while the row above re-flows and moves it, so the row that moves it is the second
+// subject; the ink is still the only thing measured.
+useResizeObserver([mastheadEl, () => wordmarkEl()], publishMastheadFoot);
+
+// AN OPEN ROW, MEASURED AND NOT CURED HERE — AND NOW ASSERTED RATHER THAN ASSURED. WebKit at
+// both DESK rungs publishes once and never again: the token reads 101.34 against an ink bottom
+// of 117.75 — 16.41px stale, 16.58px at 1440 — and one resize nudge corrects it to 117.75
+// exactly, so the callback ran before the wordmark's last growth rather than the box continuing
+// to move. Chromium is 0.00 at every rung and WebKit is 0.00 at every rung BELOW 1024.
+// Pass 2 closed that gap with a SENTENCE: "nothing reads the stale value today, because
+// `--sheet-chrome` is a `<1024` rule". A sentence about a media query is exactly the kind of
+// claim that goes quiet when someone adds a rule — so pass 3 makes it a row. `zone-grammar
+// .spec.ts` walks `document.styleSheets` and asserts that EVERY rule declaring or reading
+// `--sheet-chrome` sits under a `max-width: 1023.98px` condition: NO DESK RUNG SPENDS
+// `--sheet-chrome`. The day one does, the staleness stops being booked and starts being a red,
+// which is the right order. Two re-publishes on clocks this estate already waits for (a frame
+// after mount, `fonts.ready`) were auditioned and measured INERT — the value was already final
+// when both ran.
+
 function closeAll() {
   desktopAttribution.value?.close();
   mobileAttribution.value?.close();
